(Title) Tj
T*
(Effects of Electrical Noise Stimulation on Postural Control During Dynamic Balance) Tj
T*
(Challenges in Parkinson Disease) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Gait instability in Parkinson disease reflects impaired sensorimotor integration and) Tj
T*
(dysfunctional balance mechanisms. Electrical stimulation delivering sub-sensory-threshold) Tj
T*
(noise may enhance sensory system responsiveness. We evaluated whether such stimulation) Tj
T*
(improves postural stability during visually-challenging walking in 14 individuals with) Tj
T*
(Parkinson disease. Participants completed walking trials within a virtual environment) Tj
T*
(delivering dynamic visual perturbations, performed with and without individualized) Tj
T*
(electrical noise stimulation. We measured center-of-mass sway, foot placement precision,) Tj
T*
(and ankle stabilizing responses bilaterally. Peak center-of-mass lateral excursion was) Tj
T*
(significantly increased during noise stimulation compared with the no-stimulation) Tj
T*
(condition \(mean difference 3.2 cm; p=0.02\), indicating reduced postural stability.) Tj
T*
(Paradoxically, the stimulation amplified rather than dampened the destabilizing effect of) Tj
T*
(visual perturbations. Foot placement control metrics and ankle torque responses showed no) Tj
T*
(significant between-condition differences. Exploratory analysis suggested the stimulation-) Tj
T*
(induced increase in sensory noise overwhelmed compensatory balance mechanisms in this) Tj
T*
(population. These findings indicate electrical noise stimulation may be counterproductive) Tj
T*
(for gait stability in Parkinson disease.) Tj
ET
